#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"}] :question/id #uuid "b4649e26-6d7b-5c3e-9c8e-d55a9db5eb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5797" :question/problem "Given that $x=2\\textrm{i}$ is a solution to the quartic equation $x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36 = 0$, find all of the solutions." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x) = x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{2\\textrm{i}}=-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-2\\text{i}\\big)\\big(x-(-2\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) & =(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2+4$ using long division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+9 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+13{x}^{2}+36\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,9{x}^{2}+36 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{9{x}^{2}+36}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2+4)(x^2+9).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve $x^2+9 = 0{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=\\pm 2\\textrm{i}, \\, \\pm 3 \\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175735 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d8bf44c3-384b-553a-931f-a09ac2a3a649" :question/math-academy-id "e-8802" :question/problem "Given that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are solutions to the equation $x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13 = 0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13.$ Since $x_1=-1$ and $x_2=1$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2-1$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-6x+13 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+6x-13\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+6x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+6x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,-13 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,13{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,-13}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2-1)(x^2-6x+13).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $x^2-6x+13=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+13 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+13 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\pm 1$ and $x=3\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984041c4-abd0-5440-bb36-6ecff6e054bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 25\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353052} {:answer/id #uuid "73c92e88-9a19-569d-bc79-dc6a10dba006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353053} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a5e59e-fcb5-5a03-bf14-7b27d9f1ed03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353054} {:answer/id #uuid "71378468-f337-56c8-8759-a0a95a6e75cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353055} {:answer/id #uuid "f30e73a2-70f4-5028-8958-6082497bca2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71378468-f337-56c8-8759-a0a95a6e75cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353055} :answer-field/id #uuid "929217c4-f822-5cf7-b5b7-e656cf8f9db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5271c188-8661-5043-9643-6a802470bc9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106473" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25=0.$ Given that $x=-1$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=-1$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we can use long division to find the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+25 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,25{x}^{2}+50x+25 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{25{x}^{2}+50x+25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+2x+1)({x}^{2}+25).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+25=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-1,\\,-1,\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2256a567-7e90-58d1-920d-92121836e8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353059} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3c26d7-08cd-5263-a256-85bc0759c88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm \\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353060} {:answer/id #uuid "45bc77f1-f169-5309-85c1-a313f298519e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353061} {:answer/id #uuid "e99f5ccc-fe95-51a6-acdb-b035213f70c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353062} {:answer/id #uuid "ebedc2a0-eac3-5381-983c-fb5714753073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2256a567-7e90-58d1-920d-92121836e8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353059} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4154bb-8b88-5594-a006-fe48ad9e0ff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89f671c1-5437-587a-bbe4-3f2723125704" :question/math-academy-id "q-106479" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40=0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i})x-(1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-2x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-2x+10)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f504df29-a41d-503a-8764-cea97692318f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\pm 9\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353066} {:answer/id #uuid "63b9c345-9198-50c4-a9dc-345123737ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353067} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cc3f7b-1637-5cf9-a0fe-002c8ef4ddc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353068} {:answer/id #uuid "598c9a95-cb91-5d13-9da0-a339d4b01897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353069} {:answer/id #uuid "630d5039-b743-501b-b713-ea844a1e8700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pm 5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63b9c345-9198-50c4-a9dc-345123737ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353067} :answer-field/id #uuid "5670ccdc-6008-507e-b4b8-b1a143e76f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d7e4b56-db47-58d0-8031-6bcb9e31279c" :question/math-academy-id "q-108275" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27=0.$ Given that $x=-3$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=-3$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}={x}^{2}+6x+9\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we can use long division to find the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+6x+9\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27\\,\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{2}+18x+27 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{2}+18x+27}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x)=({x}^{2}+6x+9)({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+3=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=-3,\\,-3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afc1b30f-bb05-53c9-b014-b68255c3c7dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353073} {:answer/id #uuid "dbffe8e0-162c-5c24-a626-99f27b573d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,1,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353074} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9dc29-06e7-505e-bbc1-5cf3011a2cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,2,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353075} {:answer/id #uuid "90f0b144-ff3f-5753-991e-7ed10ec56770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,\\pm 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353076} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0c3157-8d48-57d4-8517-7590e0dd28b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,2,\\,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbffe8e0-162c-5c24-a626-99f27b573d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,1,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353074} :answer-field/id #uuid "e337651d-641c-581b-b3a5-5f9c278d73f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e93a60-e0d2-53ca-b17e-93116ba4ddd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-108344" :question/problem "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25=0$ given that $x=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}$ is a solution." :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}}=-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})) & =(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-(\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+5$ using long division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-6x+5\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-30x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}-30x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5{x}^{2}+25 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{2}+25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+5)({x}^{2}-6x+5).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-6x+5=0:$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,1,\\,5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b90041d-ad52-52f9-8ca7-6c34382aa89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353080} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6ef34b-f52d-5cf5-ab47-35ae352c50ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353081} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaf0ccc-f884-57e8-ab0f-a02a2fcd31d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353082} {:answer/id #uuid "53cb9d77-0613-550a-aae5-91ef5af1f21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353083} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6bc736-2e7a-5809-98f3-3cb762bb2d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b90041d-ad52-52f9-8ca7-6c34382aa89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353080} :answer-field/id #uuid "04cf82de-d5b3-574c-a70f-3bfe0e8bd5b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9573c94-df1a-5364-a142-d39ece50c7ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-17317" :question/problem "Given that $x=-1$ and $x=4$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16=0,$ find the remaining solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16.$ Since ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=4$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-4)={x}^{2}-3x-4\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-3x-4$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-3x-4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,-12x-16\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-12x-16 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}-12x-16}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-3x-4)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-1,\\,4,\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ac99233-d654-54df-b98a-fae08263c40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac99233-d654-54df-b98a-fae08263c40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353087} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcd5f408-cbb5-51f6-8c77-8ca0a5646333" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34834d6b-04c8-54ba-b113-1fec99617447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34834d6b-04c8-54ba-b113-1fec99617447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353089} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ffc185c-ff61-501d-b003-a87397fa2f6f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72e0bfe3-3c9b-570f-92cc-8502b1b87ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e0bfe3-3c9b-570f-92cc-8502b1b87ee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353091} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8164fea-66b5-582b-92f0-edfebbfa6bb1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34554b63-1e58-5bc9-84fc-cae5cbe8cc7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-275146" :question/problem "Given that $x=4-\\text{i}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}-72x+153=0,$ find the remaining solutions in the form $a\\pm b\\,\\text{i},$ where $b$ is **non-negative** number.\n\n$x=4\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-3}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}-72x+153.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=4-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}}=4+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(4+\\text{i}))(x-(4-\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(4+\\text{i}))(x-(4-\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(4-\\text{i})x-(4+\\text{i})x+(4-\\text{i})(4+\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+({4}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-8x+17$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+17 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}-72x+153\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+17{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,9{x}^{2}-72x+153 \\\\  & \\,{9{x}^{2}-72x+153}_{―} \\\\  & \\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-8x+17)({x}^{2}+9).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+9=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=4-\\text{i},$ are\n$x=4+1\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=0\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2a73451-fb53-5e86-82cf-483e775bd2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a73451-fb53-5e86-82cf-483e775bd2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353094} :answer-field/id #uuid "667edd3c-8036-5ba4-8ca4-63bb20ba51dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "299ace7f-e200-5f1c-a46e-77fd6d0ac779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "299ace7f-e200-5f1c-a46e-77fd6d0ac779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353096} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cc27ecc-33dd-5c56-86a3-62cb839c7c79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8fd343f-e7a6-5d26-80ed-7e7496790c4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-320869" :question/problem "Given that $x=3$ and $x=-2$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x-30=0,$ find the remaining solutions in the form $a\\pm b\\,\\text{i},$ where $b$ is **non-negative** number.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x-30.$ Since ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=-2$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+2)={x}^{2}-x-6\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-x-6$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}-2x+5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x-30\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,-2{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+7x-30 \\\\  & \\,{\\,-\\,2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+12x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,5{x}^{2}-5x-30 \\\\  & \\,{5{x}^{2}-5x-30}_{―} \\\\  & \\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-x-6)({x}^{2}-2x+5).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-2x+5=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+5 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+5-1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=3$ and $x=-2,$ are $x=1\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57987f7d-21e1-51cb-a745-a24f2b257f11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given Two Real Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9764f011-8fd9-5cef-99a1-a8fcd9bef234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353099} {:answer/id #uuid "62992a44-726a-5fc9-b7ed-c7d0d7ff16de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62992a44-726a-5fc9-b7ed-c7d0d7ff16de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353100} :answer-field/id #uuid "06b5b90d-5fc7-5ce9-968d-39a20f777814" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47262dd7-1d57-54fb-b9eb-3a71a9aa68ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-320894" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+20{x}^{2}-64x+64=0.$ Given that $x=2$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions in the form $\\pm b\\,\\text{i},$ where $b$ is **non-negative** number.\n\n$x=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+20{x}^{2}-64x+64.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=2$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}={x}^{2}-4x+4\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-4x+4$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+16 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-4x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+20{x}^{2}-64x+64\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,16{x}^{2}-64x+64 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{16{x}^{2}-64x+64}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+4)({x}^{2}+16).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+16=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from ${x}_{1,2}=2,$ are $x=\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c9ed73a-e751-5ad2-bc36-97dcc01dd7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353103} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fc8caf-a341-5a84-8420-bd0457970103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c9ed73a-e751-5ad2-bc36-97dcc01dd7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353103} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c02424d-6ee6-5941-afbf-5fbb7db3a704" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353102} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af2b63f7-4e3d-5290-9554-3680d9ad7324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af2b63f7-4e3d-5290-9554-3680d9ad7324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353106} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ae6a964-eb4f-568f-9e84-c67d0025913b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8e840a-5353-5719-b390-88cb4f8d63cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b8e840a-5353-5719-b390-88cb4f8d63cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353108} :answer-field/id #uuid "78d20676-36a2-55d3-8f57-cb8b54848de1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6836fb63-08aa-5b61-910d-fc3b1851cd9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-320923" :question/problem "Given that $x=3-4\\,\\text{i}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100=0,$ find the remaining solutions.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i},$ \n and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-4\\,\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-4\\,\\text{i}}=3+4\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-(3-4\\,\\text{i}))(x-(3+4\\,\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-4\\,\\text{i}))(x-(3+4\\,\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(3+4\\,\\text{i})x-(3-4\\,\\text{i})x+(3-4\\,\\text{i})(3+4\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+25 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}+76x \\\\  & \\,{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+100x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-24x+100 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}-24x+100}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+25)({x}^{2}+4x+4).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4x+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=3-4\\,\\text{i},$ are\n$x=3+4\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7ca77fd-e6ef-568f-be32-e1ae47c3d8dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All the Roots of a Quartic Equation Given One Complex Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea7c37c8-4b7d-5afa-bf39-53075fbffd58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353111} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4957c1-860a-5b3e-aa67-286f25a7b2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee4957c1-860a-5b3e-aa67-286f25a7b2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353112} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5c42a0b-bef6-585d-bb9f-d31f53da6138" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b30c5d5f-92e3-5d1d-965f-fff074a6d785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b30c5d5f-92e3-5d1d-965f-fff074a6d785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353114} :answer-field/id #uuid "d848931e-e082-5f22-9363-5e4b5b1e6a4e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a33f893-8b72-575e-af0f-c4671572a6bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a33f893-8b72-575e-af0f-c4671572a6bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353116} :answer-field/id #uuid "49702601-6f1a-5438-9d83-3c2c857e6f94" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06a49a00-3e22-5632-b46d-75a4b7ec6115" :question/math-academy-id "q-320934" :question/problem "Given that $x=-2-5\\text{i}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377=0,$ find the remaining solutions in the form $a\\pm b\\,\\text{i},$ where $b$ is **non-negative** number.\n\n$x=-2\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-3}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-2-5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-2-5\\text{i}}=-2+5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-2+5\\text{i}))(x-(-2-5\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-2+5\\text{i}))(x-(-2-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(-2-5\\text{i})x-(-2+5\\text{i})x+(-2-5\\text{i})(-2+5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+((-2)^{2}-(5\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+4x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}-6x+13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+29 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+29{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,-6{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-122x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}-174x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}+52x+377 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{13{x}^{2}+52x+377}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+4x+29)({x}^{2}-6x+13).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-6x+13=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+13 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+9)+13-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=-2-5\\text{i},$ are\n$x=-2+5\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.$"}]}
